1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,960
You're standing on a hill in thick fog.

2
00:00:02,960 --> 00:00:05,640
The ground under your feet is perfectly flat.

3
00:00:05,640 --> 00:00:09,200
No slope in any direction. So where are

4
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:11,280
you? Maybe at the top of the hill,

5
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:13,500
maybe at the bottom of a valley, or

6
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:17,350
somewhere stranger, a mountain pass, where the ground

7
00:00:17,350 --> 00:00:19,680
rises ahead of you and falls away on

8
00:00:19,680 --> 00:00:22,810
both sides. The slope can't tell you. Here,

9
00:00:22,810 --> 00:00:25,550
the slope is zero. To know where you

10
00:00:25,550 --> 00:00:28,000
are, you need to know how the ground

11
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:30,880
bends. And here's a puzzle. This surface curves

12
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:33,680
upward if you walk east. It curves upward

13
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:35,880
if you walk north. And yet, the point

14
00:00:35,880 --> 00:00:38,410
in the middle is not a valley. The

15
00:00:38,410 --> 00:00:40,440
answer lives in a small grid of four

16
00:00:40,440 --> 00:00:43,820
numbers. It's called the Hessian Matrix. Over the

17
00:00:43,820 --> 00:00:45,920
next few minutes, we'll build it from nothing,

18
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:48,120
see what each number means, and see why

19
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:51,130
the same idea helps computer search landscapes with

20
00:00:51,130 --> 00:00:54,280
millions of dimensions. All you need is the

21
00:00:54,280 --> 00:00:56,750
idea of a slope. Let's start with a

22
00:00:56,750 --> 00:00:59,930
single curve. Why equals x squared? At every

23
00:00:59,930 --> 00:01:02,330
point, it has a slope. That's the first

24
00:01:02,330 --> 00:01:05,200
derivative. On the left, the slope is negative.

25
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:07,430
On the right, it's positive, and right at

26
00:01:07,430 --> 00:01:10,520
the bottom, it's zero. Now, watch the slope

27
00:01:10,520 --> 00:01:12,400
itself. As we move to the right, it

28
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:15,340
keeps rising steadily. The rate at which the

29
00:01:15,340 --> 00:01:17,970
slope changes is the second derivative. For x

30
00:01:17,970 --> 00:01:21,890
squared, it's two everywhere. A positive second derivative

31
00:01:21,890 --> 00:01:24,670
means the slope is rising. The curve bends

32
00:01:24,670 --> 00:01:27,140
upward like a smile. Flip it over to

33
00:01:27,140 --> 00:01:29,940
minus x squared, and the second derivative is

34
00:01:29,940 --> 00:01:32,410
minus two. The curve bends down like a

35
00:01:32,410 --> 00:01:35,730
frown. So the second derivative measures bending, and

36
00:01:35,730 --> 00:01:37,810
that's exactly what we need at a flat

37
00:01:37,810 --> 00:01:40,900
spot. Take cosine of x. At zero, it's

38
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:44,340
flat. Top or bottom? The second derivative there

39
00:01:44,340 --> 00:01:47,840
is minus one. It bends down. A peak.

40
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:50,270
Zooming close and any curve starts to look

41
00:01:50,270 --> 00:01:53,600
like a parabola. Near zero, cosine looks almost

42
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:56,720
exactly like one. Minus x squared over two.

43
00:01:56,780 --> 00:01:59,730
The second derivative decides which way that parabola

44
00:01:59,730 --> 00:02:02,910
opens. That's the second derivative test. Flat and

45
00:02:02,910 --> 00:02:06,520
bending up. A valley. Flat and bending down.

46
00:02:06,520 --> 00:02:09,180
A peak. And when the bending is zero,

47
00:02:09,180 --> 00:02:11,760
like x cubed at zero, the test simply

48
00:02:11,760 --> 00:02:14,200
can't tell. Now, let's step off the line

49
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:17,160
and onto a surface. A function of two

50
00:02:17,160 --> 00:02:19,320
inputs, x and y, gives a height above

51
00:02:19,320 --> 00:02:22,480
every point on the floor. Together, those heights

52
00:02:22,480 --> 00:02:24,700
form a landscape. To measure slope on a

53
00:02:24,700 --> 00:02:27,630
landscape, we slice it. Hold y fixed and

54
00:02:27,630 --> 00:02:30,230
cut along the x direction. The cut is

55
00:02:30,230 --> 00:02:32,530
an ordinary curve and its slope is the

56
00:02:32,530 --> 00:02:35,570
partial derivative with respect to x. Cut along

57
00:02:35,570 --> 00:02:37,600
y instead and you get the partial derivative

58
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:40,240
with respect to y. Put those two slopes

59
00:02:40,240 --> 00:02:41,920
together and you get an arrow on the

60
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:45,500
floor, the gradient. It points straight uphill and

61
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:48,090
its length tells you how steep. Walk around

62
00:02:48,090 --> 00:02:50,640
and the arrow changes. On a steep side,

63
00:02:50,640 --> 00:02:53,480
it's long. Near the top, it shrinks. And

64
00:02:53,480 --> 00:02:56,570
at a few special points, it vanishes. These

65
00:02:56,570 --> 00:02:59,320
flat points are called critical points. Look at

66
00:02:59,320 --> 00:03:02,760
these three surfaces. A bowl, a dome, and

67
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:05,140
a saddle, shaped like a mountain pass. At

68
00:03:05,140 --> 00:03:07,470
the center of each one, the gradient is

69
00:03:07,470 --> 00:03:09,970
zero. But the bowl is a valley. The

70
00:03:09,970 --> 00:03:11,970
dome is a peak and the saddle is

71
00:03:11,970 --> 00:03:14,500
neither. It's low one way and high the

72
00:03:14,500 --> 00:03:16,680
other way. To tell them apart, we need

73
00:03:16,680 --> 00:03:19,480
second derivatives again. But on a surface, there's

74
00:03:19,480 --> 00:03:22,060
more than one. We have two slopes, the

75
00:03:22,060 --> 00:03:24,360
slope in x and the slope in y.

76
00:03:24,420 --> 00:03:26,800
And each slope can change in two ways.

77
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:28,920
As you move in x or as you

78
00:03:28,920 --> 00:03:32,200
move in y. Two slopes, two directions, four

79
00:03:32,200 --> 00:03:35,420
second derivatives. How the x slope changes as

80
00:03:35,420 --> 00:03:37,880
you move in x. That's the bending of

81
00:03:37,880 --> 00:03:40,190
the x slice. We write it f x

82
00:03:40,190 --> 00:03:42,700
x. How the y slope changes as you

83
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:44,920
move in y. The bending of the y

84
00:03:44,920 --> 00:03:47,740
slice, f y y. The other two are

85
00:03:47,740 --> 00:03:51,080
more interesting. Picture yourself on the surface facing

86
00:03:51,080 --> 00:03:53,130
east. Now, take a step to the side

87
00:03:53,130 --> 00:03:55,760
toward the north. Does the ground ahead of

88
00:03:55,760 --> 00:03:58,390
you get steeper or flatter? That's f x

89
00:03:58,390 --> 00:04:02,210
y. It measures twist. The surface z equals

90
00:04:02,210 --> 00:04:05,100
x times y shows this perfectly. Along the

91
00:04:05,100 --> 00:04:07,700
x-axis, it's flat. But step north and the

92
00:04:07,700 --> 00:04:11,060
ground facing east tilts upward. Step south and

93
00:04:11,060 --> 00:04:13,640
it tilts down. It's bending in x is

94
00:04:13,640 --> 00:04:16,410
zero. It's bending in y is zero. But

95
00:04:16,410 --> 00:04:19,120
it's twist is one. The last one, f

96
00:04:19,120 --> 00:04:21,600
y x, asks the same question the other

97
00:04:21,600 --> 00:04:24,780
way around. For any smooth surface, it always

98
00:04:24,780 --> 00:04:27,020
matches f x y. The twist is the

99
00:04:27,020 --> 00:04:29,920
same from both sides. Now arrange all four

100
00:04:29,920 --> 00:04:32,280
in a square. Bending in x on the

101
00:04:32,280 --> 00:04:34,320
top left, bending in y on the bottom

102
00:04:34,320 --> 00:04:36,370
right, and the twist in the other two

103
00:04:36,370 --> 00:04:39,500
corners. That's the Hessian matrix, named after the

104
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:42,920
German mathematician Otto Hess. Because the twist appears

105
00:04:42,920 --> 00:04:45,640
twice, the grid is symmetric. A mirror image

106
00:04:45,640 --> 00:04:48,840
across its diagonal. Let's build one. F equals

107
00:04:48,840 --> 00:04:51,220
x squared plus three x y plus y

108
00:04:51,220 --> 00:04:54,210
squared. The bending in x is two. The

109
00:04:54,210 --> 00:04:56,620
bending in y is two. The twist is

110
00:04:56,620 --> 00:05:00,370
three. So the Hessian is two, three, three,

111
00:05:00,370 --> 00:05:02,230
two. Hold on to that one. It's the

112
00:05:02,230 --> 00:05:04,660
puzzle surface from the start. A surface can

113
00:05:04,660 --> 00:05:07,010
bend in any direction, not just east or

114
00:05:07,010 --> 00:05:09,880
north. So what about the directions in between?

115
00:05:09,880 --> 00:05:12,700
Here's the beautiful part. You don't need any

116
00:05:12,700 --> 00:05:15,860
new derivatives. Pick a direction as a little

117
00:05:15,860 --> 00:05:18,481
arrow of length one. Multiply the Hessian by

118
00:05:18,480 --> 00:05:21,180
that arrow. Then take the dot product with

119
00:05:21,180 --> 00:05:23,580
the same arrow again. The number that comes

120
00:05:23,580 --> 00:05:26,290
out is the bending in that direction. Let's

121
00:05:26,290 --> 00:05:28,980
watch it on the puzzle surface. Facing east,

122
00:05:28,980 --> 00:05:32,670
the bending is two. A smile. Now slowly

123
00:05:32,670 --> 00:05:36,870
turn. Toward the northeast, the bending grows. Three,

124
00:05:36,870 --> 00:05:40,610
four. At 45 degrees, it reaches five. The

125
00:05:40,610 --> 00:05:44,120
strongest smile on the surface. Keep turning, the

126
00:05:44,120 --> 00:05:47,290
bending falls. Back to two, facing north. And

127
00:05:47,290 --> 00:05:50,520
it keeps falling through zero and past it.

128
00:05:50,520 --> 00:05:53,800
Facing northwest, the bending is minus one, a

129
00:05:53,800 --> 00:05:56,960
frown. So the surface smiles along one diagonal

130
00:05:56,960 --> 00:05:59,720
and frowns along the other. It's a saddle.

131
00:05:59,720 --> 00:06:01,960
The east and north slices showed us two

132
00:06:01,960 --> 00:06:05,130
smiles. The frown was hiding in between. Those

133
00:06:05,130 --> 00:06:08,080
two special directions are always at right angles.

134
00:06:08,080 --> 00:06:10,770
They're called the eigenvectors of the Hessian. And

135
00:06:10,770 --> 00:06:13,320
the bending along them, five and minus one,

136
00:06:13,320 --> 00:06:16,010
are its eigenvalues. Turn the grid to line

137
00:06:16,010 --> 00:06:19,080
up with those directions and the twist disappears.

138
00:06:19,080 --> 00:06:22,100
The Hessian becomes just its two eigenvalues sitting

139
00:06:22,100 --> 00:06:24,160
on the diagonal. And this is how any

140
00:06:24,160 --> 00:06:26,950
smooth surface looks up close. The height plus

141
00:06:26,950 --> 00:06:28,890
a tilt from the gradient plus a bend

142
00:06:28,890 --> 00:06:31,360
from the Hessian. Just like the parabola from

143
00:06:31,360 --> 00:06:34,030
before, one dimension up. Now we can answer

144
00:06:34,030 --> 00:06:36,090
the question from the foggy hill. At a

145
00:06:36,090 --> 00:06:38,970
flat point, look at the two eigenvalues. Both

146
00:06:38,970 --> 00:06:42,000
positive, the surface smiles in every direction. A

147
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:45,610
valley, a local minimum. Both negative, it frowns

148
00:06:45,610 --> 00:06:49,080
in every direction. A peak, a local maximum.

149
00:06:49,080 --> 00:06:51,200
One of each, it smiles one way and

150
00:06:51,200 --> 00:06:54,150
frowns the other. A saddle. There's even a

151
00:06:54,150 --> 00:06:57,370
shortcut. Multiply the two bending numbers and subtract

152
00:06:57,370 --> 00:07:00,130
the twist squared. That's the determinant and it

153
00:07:00,130 --> 00:07:03,400
equals the two eigenvalues multiplied together. If it's

154
00:07:03,400 --> 00:07:06,560
negative, they have opposite signs. A saddle. If

155
00:07:06,560 --> 00:07:08,880
it's positive, they share a sign and the

156
00:07:08,880 --> 00:07:11,140
bending in x tells you which. For the

157
00:07:11,140 --> 00:07:14,130
puzzle surface, two times two is four. Three

158
00:07:14,130 --> 00:07:17,140
squared is nine. Four minus nine is minus

159
00:07:17,140 --> 00:07:21,440
five. A saddle, confirmed. Now a real landscape.

160
00:07:21,440 --> 00:07:24,030
F equals x cubed minus three x plus

161
00:07:24,030 --> 00:07:26,800
y squared. It has two flat points at

162
00:07:26,800 --> 00:07:29,400
x equals one and x equals minus one.

163
00:07:29,400 --> 00:07:31,600
At x equals one, the Hessian is six,

164
00:07:31,600 --> 00:07:35,470
zero, zero, two. Both positive, a valley. At

165
00:07:35,470 --> 00:07:38,330
x equals minus one, it's minus six, zero,

166
00:07:38,330 --> 00:07:41,370
zero, two. One of each, a saddle. The

167
00:07:41,370 --> 00:07:43,500
pass that leads out of the valley. And

168
00:07:43,500 --> 00:07:46,080
just like on the line, sometimes the test

169
00:07:46,080 --> 00:07:48,230
can't tell. X to the fourth plus y

170
00:07:48,230 --> 00:07:50,510
to the fourth is a valley. But at

171
00:07:50,510 --> 00:07:53,350
its center, the Hessian is all zeros. So

172
00:07:53,350 --> 00:07:55,370
is the Hessian of the monkey saddle with

173
00:07:55,370 --> 00:07:57,690
room for two legs and a tail. When

174
00:07:57,690 --> 00:08:00,370
an eigenvalue is zero, you have to look

175
00:08:00,370 --> 00:08:02,920
closer. So why does this matter outside a

176
00:08:02,920 --> 00:08:05,220
math class? Because so much of the modern

177
00:08:05,220 --> 00:08:07,420
world is about finding the bottom of a

178
00:08:07,420 --> 00:08:09,941
valley. Training a neural network means searching a

179
00:08:09,940 --> 00:08:12,790
landscape for its lowest point. The height is

180
00:08:12,790 --> 00:08:15,140
the error. And the landscape has not two

181
00:08:15,140 --> 00:08:18,520
dimensions, but millions. The simplest way down is

182
00:08:18,520 --> 00:08:21,060
to follow the gradient, one small step at

183
00:08:21,060 --> 00:08:24,320
a time. That's gradient descent. But watch it

184
00:08:24,320 --> 00:08:27,610
in a long narrow valley. Here, the eigenvalues

185
00:08:27,610 --> 00:08:30,110
are two and 20. The walls are 10

186
00:08:30,110 --> 00:08:33,240
times steeper than the floor. Each step overshoots

187
00:08:33,240 --> 00:08:36,180
and the path zigzags back and forth crawling

188
00:08:36,180 --> 00:08:39,360
toward the bottom. That ratio, 20 over two,

189
00:08:39,360 --> 00:08:41,830
is called the condition number. Now bring in

190
00:08:41,830 --> 00:08:44,620
the Hessian. Newton's method treats the ground around

191
00:08:44,620 --> 00:08:46,510
you as a bowl, built from the gradient

192
00:08:46,510 --> 00:08:48,720
and the Hessian, and jumps straight to the

193
00:08:48,720 --> 00:08:51,060
bottom of that bowl. On this valley, one

194
00:08:51,060 --> 00:08:54,440
jump lands exactly on the minimum. The catch?

195
00:08:54,440 --> 00:08:56,560
With a million inputs, the Hessian is a

196
00:08:56,560 --> 00:08:59,820
million by a million, a trillion numbers. So

197
00:08:59,820 --> 00:09:02,650
real methods only estimate pieces of it. And

198
00:09:02,650 --> 00:09:05,700
the Hessian explains something surprising. In these huge

199
00:09:05,700 --> 00:09:08,800
landscapes, most flat points aren't valleys at all.

200
00:09:08,800 --> 00:09:11,700
They're saddles. To be a valley, every single

201
00:09:11,700 --> 00:09:14,710
eigenvalue has to be positive. With millions of

202
00:09:14,710 --> 00:09:17,990
them, a few are almost always negative. There's

203
00:09:17,990 --> 00:09:20,580
almost always a way down. Let's walk back

204
00:09:20,580 --> 00:09:23,140
through it. On a line, the second derivative

205
00:09:23,140 --> 00:09:26,560
measures bending. Positive is a smile. Negative is

206
00:09:26,560 --> 00:09:28,890
a frown. On a surface, there are four

207
00:09:28,890 --> 00:09:32,000
second derivatives, bending in x, bending in y,

208
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:34,800
and the twist, which shows up twice. Arrange

209
00:09:34,800 --> 00:09:36,320
them in a grid and you have the

210
00:09:36,320 --> 00:09:38,820
Hessian. Give it any direction, and it tells

211
00:09:38,820 --> 00:09:41,420
you the bending that way. Its eigenvectors point

212
00:09:41,420 --> 00:09:44,320
along the strongest smile and the deepest frown.

213
00:09:44,320 --> 00:09:46,850
Its eigenvalues say how strong they are. And

214
00:09:46,850 --> 00:09:50,080
at a flat point, their signs decide everything.

215
00:09:50,080 --> 00:09:53,520
All positive, a valley, all negative, a peak,

216
00:09:53,520 --> 00:09:56,150
mixed, a saddle. So the next time you're

217
00:09:56,150 --> 00:09:59,140
standing somewhere flat, in the fog, you'll know

218
00:09:59,140 --> 00:10:01,400
what to ask, not which way is down,

219
00:10:01,400 --> 00:10:05,320
but how the ground bends. Thanks for watching.

